    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> turned his father's hunting lodge into a palace after seeing Nicolas Fouquet's Vaux-le-Vicomte in 1661, recruiting its architect, painter and garden designer, and the court lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> from 1682 until 6 October 1789.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> made the palace the de facto capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by 1687, drawing the nobility there to seek prestige and royal patronage within a strict court etiquette, which eroded their provincial power bases.</li><li>The Revolution emptied the palace and sold its contents in seventeen thousand lots; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> made it a history museum, and restoration since 1892 has aimed to bring it back to its state in 1789.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s eight-foot-high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> throne in the Salon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> was melted down in 1689 to help pay the costs of an expensive war, and was replaced by a more modest throne of gilded wood.</p></aside>
    </section>

