    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1623</span><span class="rf-text">Louis XIII built a modest brick-and-stone hunting lodge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1668</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> decided to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> a full-scale residence and began encasing his father's château in Le Vau's enveloppe</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1682</span><span class="rf-text">The king, the court and the government settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/womens-march-on-versailles">Women's March on Versailles</a> forced the royal family to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the palace was closed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1793</span><span class="rf-text">The Convention ordered the royal property in the palace sold at auction, a sale that ran until 1794</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1837</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> inaugurated the Museum of the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the palace</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, was signed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text">The Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> restoration project began with the replanting of the gardens</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiv.jpg" alt="Louis XIV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_XIV_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyacinthe Rigaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> is first mentioned in 1038 in a charter of the Abbey of Saint-Père de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held small villages, farmland and woodland, and in the 16th century the Gondi family, influential at court, owned the land. Louis XIII acquired part of it in 1623 and bought the remainder in 1632. In 1623 he built a modest brick-and-stone hunting lodge there, about 35 metres long, which contemporaries mocked as the "chétif château" ("puny château") and the "château de cartes" ("house of cards"). From 1631 to 1634 the architect Philibert Le Roy replaced the lodge with a château.</p><p>After Louis XIII died in 1643 his château was abandoned, and Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, regent for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, moved the court back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fronde">The Fronde</a>, a series of revolts against royal authority from 1648 to 1653, left Louis XIV determined to rule alone. After Mazarin's death in 1661 he reformed his government to exclude his mother and the princes of the blood and ordered the expansion of his father's château into a palace. On 17 August 1661 he had been a guest at a festival given by Nicolas Fouquet at Vaux-le-Vicomte, where he saw the work of Louis Le Vau, André Le Nôtre and Charles Le Brun. He imprisoned Fouquet that September and recruited the three for his own projects.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Palace of Versailles&#8221; cites where it covers History, Role in politics and culture, 19th century – history museum and government venue, 20th century:</p><ul><li>Colin Jones, Versailles (Basic Books, 2018)</li><li>Donna Bohanan, Crown and Nobility in Early Modern France (Palgrave Macmillan, 2001)</li><li>T. C. W. Blanning, The Culture of Power and the Power of Culture: Old Regime Europe 1660–1789 (Oxford University Press, 2002)</li><li>Robert W. Berger, A Royal Passion: Louis XIV as Patron of Architecture (Cambridge University Press, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors.jpg" alt="Hall of Mirrors" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chateau_Versailles_Galerie_des_Glaces.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The palace is a vast ensemble of courtyards and buildings designed to maintain architectural harmony. It covers about 63,154 square metres and has roughly 2,300 rooms. The eastern front is U-shaped, with the corps de logis and symmetrical advancing wings ending in the Dufour Pavilion to the south and the Gabriel Pavilion to the north, which enclose the Royal Court. Two larger asymmetrical wings flank it, giving a façade 402 metres long. The roofs cover about 10 hectares, and the palace has 2,143 windows, 1,252 chimneys and 67 staircases. Its earliest part, built for Louis XIII in brick, marble and slate, was surrounded in the 1660s by Le Vau's Enveloppe, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> Italian villas.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a>, at the western end, is clad in marble and has 578 mirrors facing 17 windows. Le Brun's ceiling fresco shows the first 18 years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s reign in 30 scenes, 17 of them military victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. The Salon of War and the Salon of Peace stand at its northern and southern ends. The State Apartments of the king and queen occupied the main floor of the château neuf; the king's was an enfilade of seven rooms, each dedicated to a planet and its Roman god, reduced to five after the Hall of Mirrors was added. The Royal Chapel, 40 metres high, combines traditional Gothic royal church architecture with the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. The estate also holds the Grand Trianon, the Petit Trianon, the Queen's Hamlet and the gardens of Le Nôtre.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Palace of Versailles&#8221; cites where it covers Architecture and plan, Subsidiary structures:</p><ul><li>Guy Walton, Louis XIV's Versailles (University of Chicago Press, 1986)</li><li>Robert W. Berger, A Royal Passion: Louis XIV as Patron of Architecture (Cambridge University Press, 1994)</li><li>Colin Jones, Versailles (Basic Books, 2018)</li><li>Thomas DaCosta Kaufmann, Court, Cloister, and City: The Art and Culture of Central Europe, 1450–1800 (University of Chicago Press, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert.jpg" alt="Jean-Baptiste Colbert" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colbert1666.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe de Champaigne, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Work at first concentrated on the gardens. In 1668 and 1669, as the gardens grew and after the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1668), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> decided to turn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> into a full-scale royal residence. From 1668 to 1671 Le Vau encased Louis XIII's château on three sides in the enveloppe, which gave an Italianate façade over the gardens but kept the courtyard façade, a mix of styles that dismayed Louis XIV and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> called a "patchwork". Jean-Baptiste Colbert managed the royal artisans and acted as intermediary with the king, who personally directed and inspected the planning and construction. Le Vau died in 1670, and his assistant François d'Orbay succeeded him at Versailles.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a> began in 1672 and funding was cut until 1674. After the war ended in a French victory in 1678, Jules Hardouin-Mansart became First Architect, with a restored budget and a large workforce of former soldiers. He added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> from 1678 to 1681, the Ministers' Wings, the stables, the Grand Commun and two new wings for the court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> stopped work from 1688 until 1698, and the War of the Spanish Succession, with poor harvests in 1693–94 and 1709–10, led Louis XIV to cut funding and cancel planned work. The chapel was built from 1699 to 1710. The landscape was made from a bog using methods usually employed in fortress building, and water came from the damming of the river Bièvre in the 1660s and a pumping station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> near Marly-le-Roi in 1681.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Palace of Versailles&#8221; cites where it covers Construction:</p><ul><li>Guy Walton, Louis XIV's Versailles (University of Chicago Press, 1986)</li><li>Colin Jones, Versailles (Basic Books, 2018)</li><li>Robert W. Berger, A Royal Passion: Louis XIV as Patron of Architecture (Cambridge University Press, 1994)</li><li>William Doyle (ed.), Old Regime France (Oxford University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xvi.jpg" alt="Louis XVI" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine-Fran%C3%A7ois_Callet_-_Louis_XVI,_roi_de_France_et_de_Navarre_(1754-1793),_rev%C3%AAtu_du_grand_costume_royal_en_1779_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Antoine-François Callet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> to promote himself, first with nighttime festivals in its gardens in 1664, 1668 and 1674, which print and engravings spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From 1678 especially he sought to make it his seat of government. The court moved in 1682, though not officially, since opinion of Versailles was mixed among the nobility. By 1687 it was evident to all that Versailles was the de facto capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Louis XIV attracted the nobility there to seek prestige and royal patronage within a strict court etiquette, which eroded their traditional provincial power bases. He received the Doge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in 1685, an embassy from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> in 1686 and one from Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1715.</p><p>Court ceremony centred on the king's apartments, where the lever and the coucher, his waking up and going to bed, were attended by a crowd of courtiers, and at the Grand Couvert courtiers watched Louis XIV dine alone. After his death in 1715 the court left under the regency; the move broke the cultural power of Versailles, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> courtiers spent their leisure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The seven-year-old Mozart played there at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1763. The palace and its grounds had great influence on architecture and horticulture from the mid-17th century to the end of the 18th, seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-wren">Christopher Wren</a>'s work at Hampton Court Palace, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Palace, the Palace of La Granja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> Palace and Esterházy Palace, among others.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The royal family left on 6 October 1789 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/womens-march-on-versailles">Women's March on Versailles</a>, and the palace was closed. In 1792 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> ordered the paintings and sculptures sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, and in 1793 it ordered the royal property sold at auction. The sale ran from 25 August 1793 to 11 August 1794 and disposed of the furniture, mirrors, baths and kitchen equipment in seventeen thousand lots. Fleurs-de-lys and royal emblems were chiselled off the buildings, and in 1794 the Royal Gate was destroyed and the Cour de Marbre lost its floor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> considered making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> his residence but abandoned the idea because of the cost of renovation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> ordered the royal apartments restored, but the task and cost were too great.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> began the Museum of the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1833, inaugurated on 30 June 1837 and dedicated to "all the glories of France". To make it, practically all the apartments in the main building were demolished, with only the king's and queen's left almost intact. Restoration began in 1892 under the first conservator, Pierre de Nolhac, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-d-rockefeller">John D. Rockefeller</a>, Jr. gave $2,166,000 between 1925 and 1928. A bombing by Breton terrorists in 1978 heavily damaged parts of the palace. From the 1950s the aim was to restore the palace to its state in 1789, and the museum has spent considerable funds on original furnishings. The Grand Versailles project began in 2003, after Cyclone Lothar on 26 December 1999 had destroyed over 10,000 trees in the gardens, and the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> was completed in 2006.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles.jpg" alt="Treaty of Versailles" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Versailles,_English_version.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Lloyd George, Woodrow Wilson and Georges Clemenceau, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The palace remained a stage for the state after the monarchy ended. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> it was occupied by the German general staff, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was proclaimed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-mirrors">Hall of Mirrors</a> on 18 January 1871. In March 1871 the government of the Third Republic moved in, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> meeting in the Opera, and the military operation that suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> was directed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, was signed in the Hall of Mirrors in June 1919. In 1783 the last two of the three treaties of the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, were signed at the palace.</p><p>Since the 1875 Constitution the Senate and National Assembly have met in joint session at Versailles to amend the French Constitution, as in 1999, 2000, 2003 and 2024. The palace is owned by the French state and since 1995 has been run as a Public Establishment supervised by the Ministry of Culture. The gardens of Le Nôtre became the epitome of the French formal garden style and were widely imitated. The palace is considered one of the masterpieces of French classical architecture, and it exerted major influence across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 18th and 19th centuries in architecture and the decorative arts.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Palace of Versailles grew from Louis XIII's hunting lodge of 1623 into the vast residence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> built, and the court lived there from 1682 to 1789. After the Revolution it became a history museum and a venue of state, and it has been restored since 1892.</em></p>

